# Changing Attributes

Recall from Module 1 of Course 1 that an element's **attributes** give more information about it, controlling how it looks and how it behaves. Now we will change them from JavaScript, which lets us do things like swap the image a page is showing, or disable a form control after it has been used.

Written in HTML, an attribute is a name and a value inside the opening tag, and an element can have as many as it needs:

```html
<img src="images/cat.jpg" alt="A cat">
```

Here `src` is one attribute name with the value `images/cat.jpg`, and `alt` is another.

## The Four Methods

Every element object has four methods for working with attributes:

- **`el.setAttribute(name, value)`** adds an attribute, or changes it if it is already there.
- **`el.getAttribute(name)`** gives back the value of that attribute.
- **`el.hasAttribute(name)`** returns a boolean: does this element have that attribute?
- **`el.removeAttribute(name)`** deletes the attribute entirely.

Here they are on an image:

```html id=page1
<img id="animal" src="images/cat.jpg" alt="A cat" width="200">
```

```{javascript autorun}
const image = document.querySelector('#animal');

console.log(image.getAttribute('src'));
console.log(image.hasAttribute('alt'));
console.log(image.getAttribute('alt'));
```
```+id=page1
```

### Attribute Values Are Always Strings

One detail that catches people out: `getAttribute` always hands back a **string**, even when the attribute looks like a number.

```html id=page2
<input type="text" maxlength="20">
```

```{javascript autorun}
const input = document.querySelector('input');

const raw = input.getAttribute('maxlength');

console.log(raw, typeof raw);
console.log(raw + 5);
```
```+id=page2
```

`raw + 5` gives `205`, not `25`, because `+` with a string concatenates, exactly as we saw back in Course 1.

To do arithmetic with it, convert it to a number first with the built-in **`parseInt`** function, which reads a whole number out of the front of a string:

```{javascript autorun}
const input = document.querySelector('input');

const maxChars = parseInt(input.getAttribute('maxlength'));

console.log(maxChars, typeof maxChars);
console.log(maxChars + 5);
```
```+id=page2
```

Now `maxChars` is the number `20` and the arithmetic works. Any time you read a numeric attribute and then do math with it, `parseInt` is the step in between.

## Changing What an Image Shows

An `<img>` element's `src` attribute says which image to display. Change the attribute and the picture on the page changes with it, which is how image carousels and galleries work.

Click the button:

```html id=page3
<img id="animal" src="images/cat.jpg" alt="A cat" width="200">
<button>I like dogs</button>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const image = document.querySelector('#animal');
const button = document.querySelector('button');

function changeToDog() {
  image.setAttribute('src', 'images/puppy.jpg');
  image.setAttribute('alt', 'A puppy sitting in the grass');
}

button.addEventListener('click', changeToDog);
```
```+id=page3
```

Nothing about the element was replaced; we changed one attribute value and the browser redrew. Open the inspector while you click and you can watch `src` change from `images/cat.jpg` to `images/puppy.jpg`.

Notice that we changed the `alt` text too. An image whose `src` changes but whose `alt` still describes the old picture is worse than no `alt` at all.

## Disabling a Button

Another everyday use: after someone clicks a submit button, disable it so they cannot submit twice.

```{javascript autorun}
const image = document.querySelector('#animal');
const button = document.querySelector('button');

function changeToDog() {
  image.setAttribute('src', 'images/puppy.jpg');
  image.setAttribute('alt', 'A puppy sitting in the grass');
  button.setAttribute('disabled', '');
}

button.addEventListener('click', changeToDog);
```
```+id=page3
```

Click it once and it stops responding. To re-enable it we would call `button.removeAttribute('disabled')`.

### Boolean Attributes

Look closely at that call: we set `disabled` to an empty string. Not `true`. That is not a mistake.

`disabled` is a **boolean attribute**, which means that *its presence alone* is what counts. The value is ignored. All of these disable the button just as thoroughly:

```javascript
button.setAttribute('disabled', 'true');
button.setAttribute('disabled', 'yes');
button.setAttribute('disabled', 'false');
button.setAttribute('disabled', '');
```

Yes, including `'false'`. If you want the button enabled, the attribute must not be there at all, and the only way to achieve that is `removeAttribute`. This surprises people, and it is worth remembering, because `setAttribute('disabled', 'false')` looks exactly like it should work.

## Exercises

1. Set the image's `alt` attribute to exactly `Course logo`.

    ```html id=page4
    <img id="logo" src="images/cat.jpg" width="120">
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const logo = document.querySelector('#logo');

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page4
    ```
    ```+solution
    const logo = document.querySelector('#logo');

    logo.setAttribute('alt', 'Course logo');
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#logo').should('have.attribute', 'alt').run('Add an `alt` attribute to the image with `setAttribute`', 'Added the alt attribute');
    wrap(logo.getAttribute('alt')).should('equal', 'Course logo').run('Set `alt` to exactly `Course logo`', 'Set the correct alt text');
    ```

2. The button below is disabled. Enable it.

    ```html id=page5
    <button id="save" disabled>Save</button>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const button = document.querySelector('#save');

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page5
    ```
    ```+solution
    const button = document.querySelector('#save');

    button.removeAttribute('disabled');
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(button.hasAttribute('disabled')).should('equal', false).run('The `disabled` attribute has to be removed entirely. Setting it to `false` would leave the button disabled', 'Removed the disabled attribute');
    ```

3. Make the button swap the link's destination. Clicking it should change the link's `href` to `https://www.umich.edu/` and its text to `University of Michigan`.

    ```html id=page6
    <a id="site-link" href="https://example.com/">Example</a>
    <button id="swap">Swap</button>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const link = document.querySelector('#site-link');
    const button = document.querySelector('#swap');

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page6
    ```
    ```+solution
    const link = document.querySelector('#site-link');
    const button = document.querySelector('#swap');

    function swapLink() {
      link.setAttribute('href', 'https://www.umich.edu/');
      link.textContent = 'University of Michigan';
    }

    button.addEventListener('click', swapLink);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(link.getAttribute('href')).should('equal', 'https://example.com/').run('The link should keep its original destination until the button is clicked', 'The link starts unchanged');
    assert('#swap').click().run('Could not find the `#swap` button', 'Clicked the button');
    wrap(link.getAttribute('href')).should('equal', 'https://www.umich.edu/').run('Clicking the button should set the link\'s `href` to `https://www.umich.edu/`', 'Changed the destination');
    assert('#site-link').should('have.text', 'University of Michigan').run('Clicking the button should also change the link text to `University of Michigan`', 'Changed the link text');
    ```

